# ----- Database (Neon Postgres) -----
# Copy to `.env` and replace with your Neon connection strings.
# Work against the Neon *development* branch here; production gets its own branch and its own
# values in the deployment environment.
#
# Both are on the Neon dashboard under "Connect". They differ only in the host:
#   pooled  -> ...-pooler.<region>.aws.neon.tech
#   direct  -> ...<region>.aws.neon.tech   (no "-pooler")
#
# Use `sslmode=verify-full`, not the `sslmode=require` Neon's dashboard hands you: node-postgres
# treats the two as equivalent today, but pg v9 changes what `require` verifies, and `verify-full`
# pins the stricter behaviour explicitly.

# Pooled connection, used by Prisma Client at runtime (src/lib/prisma.ts).
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST-pooler.REGION.aws.neon.tech/devstash?sslmode=verify-full"

# Direct (unpooled) connection, used by the Prisma CLI for migrations and the seed.
# Migrations take session-level locks that the PgBouncer pooler cannot serve, so this must not
# be the -pooler host.
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST.REGION.aws.neon.tech/devstash?sslmode=verify-full"

# ----- Auth (NextAuth v5) -----
# Signs and encrypts the session JWT. Generate one with `npx auth secret` or
# `openssl rand -base64 32`. Every deployment needs its own — rotating it invalidates all sessions.
AUTH_SECRET=""

# GitHub OAuth app credentials: https://github.com/settings/developers
# The provider infers these names from the `AUTH_<PROVIDER>_ID` / `_SECRET` convention.
# Callback URL: http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/github  (local)
#               https://<your-domain>/api/auth/callback/github  (production — needs its own app)
AUTH_GITHUB_ID=""
AUTH_GITHUB_SECRET=""

# ----- Email (Resend) -----
# API key: https://resend.com/api-keys
RESEND_API_KEY=""

# Sender for the verification email. Required in production: an address on a domain verified in
# Resend (Domains -> Add Domain, then publish the DNS records it hands you). Unset, `src/lib/email.ts`
# falls back to `onboarding@resend.dev`, which Resend delivers only to the account owner's own
# address — fine for local development, not for real users.
#
# Verify actual delivery with `npm run email:test -- you@example.com` rather than the API response;
# see `src/lib/email.ts` for why the response alone does not prove a send succeeded.
EMAIL_FROM="DevStash <noreply@yourdomain.com>"

# ----- Rate limiting (Upstash Redis) -----
# Throttles the rate-limited endpoints listed in `src/lib/rate-limit.ts`. Create a database at
# https://console.upstash.com and copy the *REST* URL and token from its dashboard — the SDK speaks
# HTTP, not the Redis wire protocol, which is what makes it usable from a serverless function.
#
# Optional. Missing credentials mean "not configured": every request is let through, so local
# development and CI need no Redis instance. Set both in production, or the limiter is silently off.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=""
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=""

# Origin used to build absolute verification links. A relative path is useless in an email client,
# and the request that sends the mail is not the request that will follow the link, so its host
# cannot be borrowed. NextAuth reads this too, so it must match the deployment's real origin.
# Vercel does not read a committed .env.production — set the deployed values in project settings.
AUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# ----- File storage (Cloudflare R2) -----
# Where `file` and `image` items keep their content; see `src/lib/r2.ts`. Create a bucket at
# https://dash.cloudflare.com -> R2, then an API token under "Manage R2 API Tokens" with
# Object Read & Write on it.
#
# The account id is in the R2 dashboard URL and on the bucket's settings page; the key pair is shown
# once, when the token is created.
#
# Leave the bucket **private**: every read goes through `GET /api/files/[id]`, which authorizes the
# request and streams the object back rather than exposing it at a public URL.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=""
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=""
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=""
R2_BUCKET_NAME=""

# Unused: nothing in `src/` reads it. Only meaningful for a public bucket; kept as a record for a
# future switch to public reads or presigned URLs.
R2_PUBLIC_URL=""

# ----- Billing (Stripe) -----
# The Pro subscription (src/lib/stripe.ts, src/config/billing.ts). Create the product and its two
# prices at https://dashboard.stripe.com in **test mode** first — every value below is mode-specific,
# and none of the test ones work in production.
#
# Secret key: Developers -> API keys. Server-side only; `src/lib/stripe.ts` is `server-only` so it
# can never reach the browser.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=""

# Signing secret for POST /api/stripe/webhook, which is how the handler knows a request is really
# from Stripe — that route is excluded from the proxy (src/proxy.ts) and has no session behind it.
#
# The local and deployed secrets are different values from different sources: the dashboard
# endpoint (Developers -> Webhooks) issues its own `whsec_…`, and `stripe listen` prints a separate
# one each time it runs. Put the CLI's here; put the dashboard's in the deployment's environment.
#
#   stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/stripe/webhook
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# The two recurring prices on the one "DevStash Pro" product — $8/month and $72/year. One product
# with two prices, not two products: that is what lets the Billing Portal offer a monthly<->yearly
# switch. Copy each price id (`price_…`, not the product id) from the product page.
STRIPE_PRICE_ID_MONTHLY=""
STRIPE_PRICE_ID_YEARLY=""

# Unused, like `R2_PUBLIC_URL` above: nothing in `src/` reads it. The publishable key is only needed
# by `@stripe/stripe-js` in the browser, and Checkout here is Stripe-hosted — the session is created
# server-side and the browser is redirected to `session.url`. Kept as a record for an embedded or
# custom checkout.
STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=""


# ----- AI (OpenAI) -----

# Powers the AI features (Pro only): tag suggestions today, summaries, explain, and the prompt
# optimizer after it. Read lazily in `src/lib/openai.ts`, so the app builds and runs without it —
# only the AI features refuse, with a toast, and only for the accounts entitled to reach them.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# ----- Scheduled jobs -----

# Authenticates Vercel Cron's request to `/api/cron/sweep-unverified`, which deletes registrations
# that were never confirmed. Vercel sends it as `Authorization: Bearer <secret>` on every cron
# invocation once the variable exists. The route **refuses to run** when it is unset rather than
# defaulting to open — an unauthenticated endpoint that deletes user rows is not something to leave
# to a missing variable. Any long random string; `openssl rand -base64 32` is fine.
CRON_SECRET=

# Override for `src/lib/prisma.ts`'s production guard, which refuses to connect to the production
# Neon branch from anywhere that is not the Vercel deployment. Leave unset: `npm start` sets
# NODE_ENV=production, and Next then loads `.env.production` in preference to `.env`, so this guard
# is what stops a local production-mode run from reaching the live database.
ALLOW_PRODUCTION_DB=
